  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Bambara\"\nsubtitle: \"Mali\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Bambara, also called Bamana, are a Mandé ethnic group. |\n| Where | Mainly southern Mali, with communities in Ivory Coast, Guinea, Burkina Faso and Senegal. |\n| How many | They are Mali’s largest Mandé ethnic group; about 80% of Mali’s population speaks Bambara. |\n| Language | Bambara, also called Bamana, Bamanankan or Malian. |\n| Religion | Most Bamana follow Islam while many also keep ancestor-honouring rituals. |\n| Known for | Bogolanfini mud cloth · Tyi Warra agricultural headdresses · Ritual masks and wooden figures · Griots called Jeliw · Age-group and farming societies |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Bambara are a Mandé people whose history is linked to farming communities, Ségou and the Bamana Empire. Their culture includes caste and age-group societies, ritual sculpture, masks, textiles, music objects and wooden figures. Much Bambara art was made for religious use, ceremonies and offerings. Oral traditions are carried mainly by griots, called Jeliw.\n\n## Material culture\n\nBambara material culture includes cotton textiles, ritual masks, wooden figures, locks, musical instruments, headdresses and iron objects, often made for ceremony or spiritual use.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nBambara textiles are often made from narrow woven cotton strips joined together and decorated with mud or discharge dyeing.\n\n- **Mud cloth** (*Bogolanfini*) — Bogolanfini is hand-spun cotton cloth decorated with mud resist patterns such as dashes, crosses, circles, zig-zags, stripes, diamonds, triangles and squares.\n- **Bambara mud cloth** (*Bokolan fini*) — This cotton textile is made from narrow strips sewn together before dyeing and is usually white and dark brown-black.\n- **Discharge-dyed wrapper** — These cotton wrappers use brown discharge-dyed patterns, including waves, repeating ovals and dense geometric designs.\n- **Indigo-dyed blanket** (*munnyuure*) — A munnyuure blanket is woven from ten wide strips with indigo and white bands and extra white-weft patterns.\n- **Carrying cloth** — This cloth is made from four narrow strips and has mud-resist sections forming a central diamond-chain pattern.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nDocumented Bambara dress includes cotton wrappers, skirts, blankets, carrying cloths and a masquerade costume.\n\n- **Woman’s wrapper** — Women’s wrappers may be single-width or made from seven or eight woven strips, with fringes, brown or yellow dyeing, and mud or discharge patterns.\n- **Married woman’s skirt** — This hand-woven cotton skirt has a five-by-eight grid of crosses, chevrons, zig-zags and triangles.\n- **Masquerade costume** — This cotton costume is made from 22 narrow strips and has a drawstring neckline, sleeves and two lower sleeves for the legs.\n- **Cotton garment from Ségou** — This garment measures 185 by 90 centimetres and has dark-brown discharge-dyed patterns.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nBamana everyday and artistic objects include locks, loom tools, musical instruments, calabashes and poisoned arrows.\n\n- **Human-shaped lock** — Some wooden and metal locks are carved as stylised human beings.\n- **Crocodile lock** — One lock is carved in the shape of a crocodile.\n- **Woman’s-head heddle pulley** — A wooden and metal loom pulley is carved as a woman’s head.\n- **Slit-gong** — This wooden instrument has a carved human head at one end and metal studs for eyes.\n- **Poisoned arrows** — These arrows have cane shafts, cane binding and triangular iron arrowheads.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nThe sources mention neck ornaments, earrings, nose ornaments, metal teeth, shells, seeds and scarification on objects and figures.\n\n- **Bead neck ornament** — Bead neck ornaments are shown or mentioned in the sources.\n- **Metal earrings** — Metal earrings are among the recorded forms of adornment.\n- **Nose ornament** — Nose ornaments are mentioned in the sources.\n- **Scarification pattern** — Bambara statues are described with marked or powerful scarification patterns.\n- **Cowrie and abrus decoration** — Cowrie shells and abrus seeds appear on objects and headdresses.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nBambara music uses whistles, guitars, rattles, slit-gongs and trumpets, and it is closely linked with oral performance.\n\n- **Wooden whistle or flute** — A wooden whistle or flute is among the recorded Bambara sound-making objects.\n- **Six-string guitar** — This wooden guitar has six strings and a reptile-skin resonator.\n- **Calabash rattle** — A calabash rattle or sistrum is mounted on a bent stick and topped with a human head.\n- **Slit-gong** — A scraped wooden slit-gong is used as a musical object.\n- **Dance trumpet** — A wooden trumpet was used to summon people to dance.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nBamana dance is tied to farming ceremonies, initiation and masquerade, especially through animal-form headdresses and masks.\n\n- **Antelope harvest headdress** (*Tyi Warra*) — The Tyi Warra, or ciwara, is worn at harvest time by selected young men from the farmers association.\n- **N’Tomo mask** (*n’tomo*) — The n’tomo mask was used by dancers during male initiation ceremonies.\n- **Komo head-dress mask** (*Komo*) — This mask has a clay-covered carved-wood base, an animal head, antelope horns, tusks and porcupine quills.\n- **N’Tomo society mask** (*N’Tomo*) — A wooden mask is recorded as having been used by the N’Tomo society.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources describe initiation, farming and masquerade rituals but give no annual festival calendar.\n\n- **Male initiation** (*n’tomo*) — Male initiation ceremonies used n’tomo masks and included statues made for young men.\n- **Sonkalaniw initiation figures** (*sonkalaniw*) — Sonkalaniw were made for the initiation of young men by a Jo group known as boso.\n- **Nyeleniw initiation figures** (*nyeleniw*) — Nyeleniw were used in blacksmiths’ Jo initiation.\n- **Age-group societies** (*Tons*) — Tons were age-based, sex-based or vocational associations that helped socialize people and pass on traditions.\n- **Soli ton** — The Soli ton was made up of young men who lived apart, worked on farms and later took wives.\n\n## Foodways\n\n- **Pounding and sifting meal** — One photograph shows women pounding an unidentified meal, sifting it into a calabash and carrying a child.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nBambara oral literature is mainly carried by griots, who preserve stories, praise songs, history and old songs.\n\n- **Griot** (*Jeliw*) — Jeliw are storytellers, praise singers and human history books who study singing and recitation for many years.\n- **Tales of kings and heroes** — Bambara oral literature includes many tales about kings and heroes.\n- **Songs of the old Mali empire** — Many griot songs are said to date back to the old empire of Mali.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nBambara is Mali’s main Manding lingua franca, and most Bamana combine Islam with some traditional ancestor-honouring practices.\n\n- **Bambara language** (*Bamanankan*) — Bambara, or Bamanankan, is a Manding language spoken throughout Mali as a lingua franca.\n- **N’Ko script** (*N’Ko*) — N’Ko is one of the scripts used to write Bambara.\n- **Masaba script** (*Masaba*) — Masaba was developed beginning in 1930 for writing Bambara.\n- **Ancestor honouring** — Many Bamana Muslims continue traditional rituals, especially those honouring ancestors.\n- **Syncretic Islam** — Most Bamana follow Islam while also keeping some traditional rituals.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Bogolanfini* — Mud cloth\n- *Bokolan fini* — Bambara mud cloth\n- *munnyuure* — Indigo-dyed blanket\n- *Tyi Warra* — Agricultural antelope headdress\n- *ciwara* — Another spelling of Tyi Warra\n- *n’tomo* — Male initiation mask\n- *Komo* — Mask used in ritual tradition\n- *Jeliw* — Griots and oral historians\n- *Tons* — Age, sex and vocational associations\n- *Soli ton* — Young men’s farming association\n- *sonkalaniw* — Young men’s initiation figures\n- *nyeleniw* — Blacksmiths’ initiation figures\n- *boso* — A Jo initiation group\n- *Bamanankan* — Bambara language\n- *N’Ko* — A script used for Bambara\n- *Masaba* — A script developed for Bambara\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Pascal James Imperato, “The Dance of the Tyi Wara,” *African Arts*, 1970.\n- Patrick R. McNaughton, “Bamana Blacksmiths,” *African Arts*, 1979.\n- Richard L. Roberts, *Warriors, Merchants and Slaves: The State and the Economy in the Middle Niger Valley 1700–1914*, Stanford University Press, 1987.\n- Louis Tauxier, *Histoire des Bambara*, P. Geuthner, 1942.\n- Dominique Zahan, *Antilopes du soleil: Arts et rites agraires d’Afrique noire*, Edition A. Schendl, 1980.\n- Bambara people: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bambara_people\n- Bambara art: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bambara_art\n- Bambara language: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bambara_language\n- Museum catalogue records cited above: British Museum, Museum of World Culture, Museum of Ethnography, Metropolitan Museum of Art and Cleveland Museum of Art.\n",
